doc: REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW without CONCURRENTLY is not MVCC-safe

REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW without CONCURRENTLY writes the new contents
with TABLE_INSERT_FROZEN (matview.c, transientrel_startup) and swaps them
in.  Frozen rows are visible to every snapshot, so a transaction whose
snapshot was taken before the refresh committed, and which had not yet
accessed the materialized view, reads the new contents: data from after
its snapshot.

The "Caveats" section of the MVCC chapter lists the commands that are not
MVCC-safe and does not include this one, and its description ("the table
will appear empty") does not fit it.  Document it there and on the
command's reference page.  REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY changes
the view with ordinary row operations and is not affected.

Reproducer (REL_19_0), two sessions:

    CREATE TABLE t (id int PRIMARY KEY);
    INSERT INTO t SELECT generate_series(1, 3);
    CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW mv AS SELECT id FROM t;
    INSERT INTO t VALUES (4);
    CREATE TABLE other (id int);

    s1: BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
    s1: SELECT count(*) FROM other;      -- takes the snapshot
    s2: REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW mv;
    s1: SELECT count(*) FROM mv;         -- 4, expected 3
---
--- a/doc/src/sgml/mvcc.sgml
+++ b/doc/src/sgml/mvcc.sgml
@@ -1867,6 +1867,19 @@
    </para>
 
    <para>
+    <link linkend="sql-refreshmaterializedview"><command>REFRESH
+    MATERIALIZED VIEW</command></link> without
+    the <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> option is not MVCC-safe either, in
+    a different way.  After the refresh commits, the materialized view
+    shows its new contents to concurrent transactions that are using a
+    snapshot taken before the refresh committed, provided they did not
+    access the materialized view before the refresh started.  Such a
+    transaction sees data in the materialized view that is newer than its
+    snapshot.  With the <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> option the command
+    is MVCC-safe.
+   </para>
+
+   <para>
